1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,300
Welcome to Xmip.

2
00:00:02,480 --> 00:00:07,155
Xmip stands for Cross OS Messaging & Integration Platform.

3
00:00:07,335 --> 00:00:13,760
It connects the systems an organization runs, and it keeps an account of every message it takes on.

4
00:00:13,940 --> 00:00:20,065
It is open source, written in Rust, and it runs on your own machines, with or without the internet.

5
00:00:20,245 --> 00:00:26,495
Let us follow one order through it, then see how it is configured, run, operated and extended.

6
00:00:28,675 --> 00:00:30,625
Xmip answers four people.

7
00:00:30,805 --> 00:00:35,830
The CEO asks whether the business keeps moving when an exchange fails.

8
00:00:36,010 --> 00:00:39,435
The CIO asks who controls it and where it runs.

9
00:00:39,615 --> 00:00:46,390
The developer asks how an integration is built, and the operator asks what went wrong and what to do now.

10
00:00:46,570 --> 00:00:48,295
Each answer follows.

11
00:00:50,475 --> 00:00:53,650
Here is one sales order from a trading partner.

12
00:00:53,830 --> 00:00:58,505
A Receive Location accepts it, and Xmip publishes it as a message.

13
00:00:58,685 --> 00:01:03,460
A Subscription is a filter on what a message holds, and a destination.

14
00:01:03,640 --> 00:01:13,365
The first sends the order to a Work Process that approves it; the approved order is published again, and a second Subscription sends it to the ERP.

15
00:01:13,545 --> 00:01:16,045
No filter ever names a node.

16
00:01:16,225 --> 00:01:19,425
This is the flow Xmip's own System Tests run.

17
00:01:21,605 --> 00:01:25,205
Acceptance is where responsibility changes hands.

18
00:01:25,385 --> 00:01:30,735
Before it, the stream belongs to its sender, and a refusal says where and why.

19
00:01:30,915 --> 00:01:36,415
Once Xmip accepts it, the message is written to storage before anything acts on it.

20
00:01:36,595 --> 00:01:42,470
Every journey ends Completed, Failed or Dismissed, and a failed delivery is retried.

21
00:01:42,650 --> 00:01:49,175
A message nothing wanted waits in the Dead Message Queue until its routing is fixed and it is replayed.

22
00:01:49,355 --> 00:01:51,205
Every step is audited.

23
00:01:53,385 --> 00:01:56,585
An integration in Xmip is text you can read.

24
00:01:56,765 --> 00:02:03,665
One TOML file describes the cluster: its nodes, its routes, and what each piece of work requires.

25
00:02:03,845 --> 00:02:07,270
Teams version it, compare it and review it like code.

26
00:02:07,450 --> 00:02:12,100
Every save is validated, then sliced into each node's own part.

27
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:18,155
VS Code is where an integration is drawn.

28
00:02:18,335 --> 00:02:24,935
The designer draws the same cluster file the text holds, so a change in one is a change in the other.

29
00:02:25,115 --> 00:02:32,915
Completion, validation and renaming that follows every reference come from Xmip's own language server, written in Rust.

30
00:02:33,095 --> 00:02:40,845
It speaks the standard Language Server Protocol, so other editors can use Xmip's rules through their own LSP clients.

31
00:02:43,025 --> 00:02:47,025
Where work runs is configuration, never a node's name.

32
00:02:47,205 --> 00:02:54,605
Each node declares its roles: receiving, processing, sending, or executing all three at low latency.

33
00:02:54,785 --> 00:03:03,835
It also declares its Node capabilities: words for what its environment gives it, such as a firewall opening or an installed module.

34
00:03:04,015 --> 00:03:08,665
Work names what it requires, and Xmip places it on a node that has it.

35
00:03:08,845 --> 00:03:15,445
One node with every role works; more nodes are split out as security, firewalls and load need.

36
00:03:17,625 --> 00:03:20,650
The work belongs to the cluster, not to a node.

37
00:03:20,830 --> 00:03:29,180
Every node reaches Xmip Storage for all storage, and its Ledger holds every stream, message and journey until it is done.

38
00:03:29,360 --> 00:03:34,910
Nodes work by time-limited claims, so when one dies, another picks its work up.

39
00:03:35,090 --> 00:03:39,315
A primary Storage node has standbys that follow every write.

40
00:03:39,495 --> 00:03:44,420
Stopped in order, it hands its writes over, and a standby takes over at once.

41
00:03:44,600 --> 00:03:48,175
One that vanishes is waited for, never guessed about.

42
00:03:50,355 --> 00:03:58,155
One plan runs four ways: as local processes, in containers, on virtual machines, and on physical machines.

43
00:03:58,335 --> 00:04:04,460
Virtual machines run on Hyper-V, on KVM with libvirt, or on VMware.

44
00:04:04,640 --> 00:04:10,215
The cluster file stays the same; which node runs where belongs to the environment.

45
00:04:10,395 --> 00:04:19,070
The System Tests prove the flow as processes on one computer, and the Systems Tests prove it on virtual machines, Windows and Linux.

46
00:04:19,250 --> 00:04:25,050
Each way has its own safety and security trade-offs: Xmip advises, and you choose.

47
00:04:27,230 --> 00:04:29,380
Xmip is offline by design.

48
00:04:29,560 --> 00:04:38,635
Packages are built where the internet is reachable, then brought in, and installation, operation and the runtime need no internet at all.

49
00:04:38,815 --> 00:04:43,065
Nothing at runtime checks a license or downloads a module.

50
00:04:43,245 --> 00:04:50,720
A node that may reach out says so with the Node capability online; a node that declares nothing is offline.

51
00:04:50,900 --> 00:04:53,725
The networked option is there when you choose it.

52
00:04:55,905 --> 00:05:02,905
Operators choose their surface: the xmip command line, PowerShell, the web view, the desktop or the phone.

53
00:05:03,085 --> 00:05:11,060
Each reads one shared operator model, so they cannot disagree, and an MCP client reads Xmip through the command line.

54
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:16,690
Health is said in words: Fine, Paused, Working, Stressed, Exhausted or Done.

55
00:05:16,870 --> 00:05:23,795
A scope above is Holding when something beneath it needs attention, and it carries that leaf and its evidence.

56
00:05:25,975 --> 00:05:31,675
A Report presents data Xmip already keeps, such as the Audit and the Dead Messages.

57
00:05:31,855 --> 00:05:36,980
A Quest probes an installation first, and its Report presents what it found.

58
00:05:37,160 --> 00:05:47,835
The first is the Regulation Quest, its regulation a parameter, GDPR first: for each requirement, the evidence or the gap, and the action that closes it.

59
00:05:48,015 --> 00:05:51,590
It never says compliant; that judgment stays yours.

60
00:05:51,770 --> 00:05:59,670
The Firewall, Security, Resilience and Dependency Reports sit in the same list, eleven in all, on every surface.

61
00:06:01,850 --> 00:06:04,625
Xmip is extended through one boundary.

62
00:06:04,805 --> 00:06:15,730
A module is a library a node opens through the C ABI, written in Rust, or in C# with Xmip's Module package, published as a native library.

63
00:06:15,910 --> 00:06:21,485
The node cannot tell the two apart, and a template starts a new module with its test.

64
00:06:21,665 --> 00:06:30,490
Code on another machine reaches Xmip only over its HTTP/2 API, inside Xmip's mutual TLS.

65
00:06:32,670 --> 00:06:34,670
Xmip is open source.

66
00:06:34,850 --> 00:06:44,000
Xmip itself is under the GNU Affero General Public License, version three or later, and it cannot be relicensed or withdrawn.

67
00:06:44,180 --> 00:06:54,280
Its module boundary, the headers, bindings and packages a module is built against, is under the Apache License, so the module you write stays yours.

68
00:06:54,460 --> 00:07:08,760
Every architectural decision is a record with its reasons, seventy-five so far, and tests hold the rules: the internal tests on every change, the System Tests on one computer, and the Systems Tests on machines.

69
00:07:10,940 --> 00:07:13,115
Xmip is measured, not promised.

70
00:07:13,295 --> 00:07:19,645
Its rule: anything it does around a payload that takes more than a millisecond is a defect to find.

71
00:07:19,825 --> 00:07:30,300
Carrying a file in and out on one node, Xmip's own work measured 430 microseconds at the median of twenty runs, with the storage's syncs counted apart.

72
00:07:30,480 --> 00:07:37,005
And the honest count: six transports run in the assembled service today, of the eighty-eight declared.

73
00:07:39,185 --> 00:07:49,810
That is Xmip: a message accepted once and accounted for, configuration you can read, work placed by what nodes declare, and the same answer on every surface.

74
00:07:49,990 --> 00:07:52,965
Xmip is in development, not GA yet.

75
00:07:53,145 --> 00:07:58,620
To try it, the getting-started guide carries a file in and out on your own computer.

76
00:07:58,800 --> 00:08:00,700
Thank you for watching.
